T*
(An attacker able to compromise a user?s device has full access to Clef?s encrypted back up.) Tj
T*
(Clef stores a number of files on disk, such as credentials.json containing account credentials and) Tj
T*
(config.json containing the JavaScript rule-file hashes, in order to be able to) Tj
T*
(recover from a restart. These files are encrypted by keys derived from a single seed which is) Tj
T*
(stored in cleartext on disk in the secrets.dat file.) Tj
T*
(An attacker able to access secrets.dat and credentials.json would have full and unrestricted access to) Tj
T*
(accounts and their value. Because of this, a device not protected with) Tj
T*
(disk encryption that gets physically breached would leak all information stored under Clef?s) Tj
T*
(encrypted backup. A running device that gets remotely breached would also compromise) Tj
T*
(a dormant Clef application, irrespective of disk encryption. Furthermore, an accidental nonencrypted copy) Tj
T*
( or backup of these files to a different location could compromise a user?s) Tj
T*
(accounts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Recommendation) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(6 | Sila Clef Review) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Enforce usage of a passphrase to start the Clef command-line interface. Leverage the same) Tj
T*
(passphrase mechanisms as in go-sila/accounts/keystore to protect the secrets.dat) Tj
T*
(file.) Tj
