T*
(The Clef API exposes an EcRecover method that allows to recover an Sila publickey from a signed message.) Tj
T*
(The method implements algorithm 4.1.6 \(Public Key Recovery) Tj
T*
(Operation\) from the Standards for Efficient Cryptography Group document on Elliptic Curve) Tj
T*
(Cryptography.10) Tj
T*
(As noted by the algorithm?s specification, several public keys can be recovered from a signature. This is) Tj
T*
( due to the ECDSA signature algorithm removing some information from the r) Tj
T*
(value of a signature: only the x-coordinate is retained \(2 solutions can be recovered for the) Tj
T*
(y-coordinate\) and it is further reduced modulo the order of the elliptic curve. In the case of) Tj
T*
(Sila, secp256k1 is used which has a curve order slightly lower than the field modulus,) Tj
T*
(so indeed information is lost. The curve uses a cofactor of 1, so the number of possible) Tj
T*
(solutions to the algorithm are 2 × \(1 + 1\) = 4.) Tj
T*
(In order for the recovery algorithm to recover the correct solution, a v byte is added at the) Tj
T*
(end of every Sila signature. Its least significant bit contains the sign of the y-coordinate) Tj
T*
(of the r value and the rest of the bits contain information to re-compute the x-coordinate of) Tj
T*
(the r value.) Tj
T*
(Since these bits can be tampered with, an attacker could in some cases deceive the algorithm) Tj
T*
(by leading it to a wrong public key.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Recommendation) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In order to verify the recovered key, Clef needs to:) Tj
T*
(1. Verify that the key can be used to verify the signature passed in the request. This is step) Tj
T*
(1.6.2 of the SEC algorithm which is not implemented by Clef.) Tj
T*
(2. Match the recovered public key against an Sila address or another authentication) Tj
T*
(mechanism.) Tj
T*
(To protect against these attacks, the API of Clef needs to be changed to accept an extra) Tj
T*
(?authentication? argument.) Tj
T*
